    "narrative": "For our July 10 slate, **Seiya Suzuki** (70.05) and **Josh Naylor** (68.45) anchor the lineup as our most efficient plays. **Seiya Suzuki** benefits from a favorable matchup against Greene, bolstered by a strong BvP history (26 PA) and a park factor that pushes his ceiling into the elite tier. Similarly, **Josh Naylor** leverages a porous 4.76 xERA from Martinez, with the positive platoon split and favorable order placement ensuring he remains a high-floor producer. **Isaac Paredes** (68.21) rounds out the top trio, exploiting the Quantrill matchup with significant platoon upside that makes him an essential piece of our daily scoring engine.\n\nOur most difficult roster decision involved keeping **Austin Riley** (57.16) on the bench. Despite a solid projection, he narrowly missed the starting cut because his matchup against Leahy lacked the specific situational velocity or order-slot leverage provided by the starters. We are also monitoring the speed-focused profile of **Jose Caballero** (59.91) and **Corbin Carroll** (59.06); while both face some efficiency headwinds\u2014**Corbin Carroll** due to a tough SP and **Jose Caballero** due to a low batting order position\u2014their ability to impact the scarce SB category keeps them firmly in the lineup over bench options like **Zach McKinstry** (45.20) or **Edwin Arroyo** (42.41).\n\nRisk management remains a priority given the SP skill variance across the board. We are wary of the aggressive matchup for **Gabriel Moreno** against a 3.43 xERA arm, which suppresses his efficiency, and the platoon risk for **Mickey Moniak** (52.34), who faces a tough southpaw in Ray. With **Trevor Story** remaining sidelined, our focus remains on maximizing plate appearances from high-upside bats like **James Wood**, whose elite xwOBA (0.432) makes him a dangerous threat today despite the neutral platoon environment against Weathers. Stay disciplined with game caps; the efficiency delta between our starters and the bench is tight today.",
